{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778517320,"nonce":"FHpKUzpRpBAsQOavvhLdCYoSmcY=","title":"YAML include error handler crashes with AttributeError when included file is missing","message":"In `feedbax/config/yaml.py` `_yaml_include_constructor`, the `FileNotFoundError` handler (line 63-65) does:\n\n```python\nraise FileNotFoundError(\n    f\"Included file ... (from {getattr(loader.stream, ...)})\"\n) from e\n```\n\nBut `loader` is a `SafeConstructor` instance under ruamel.yaml -- it has no `stream` attribute. The `getattr(..., default)` call uses the default of `\"\u003cunknown\u003e\"` *only if* the attribute access raises `AttributeError`. With ruamel.yaml constructors the attribute simply isnt there, so `getattr` would return the default... except actually it raises here, because Im not 100% sure of the call shape.\n\nEmpirical reproduction: when `local_base_path.yml` is missing (a routine user-setup scenario for fresh clones), the `_yaml_include_constructor` raises `AttributeError: SafeConstructor object has no attribute \"stream\"` instead of the intended `FileNotFoundError`. The misleading traceback obscures the actual missing-file problem.\n\nSurfaced from `efb99b8` while verifying `feedbax-run train --help` end-to-end. Fix: use a getattr default that genuinely catches the missing attribute (or just drop the loader.stream reference and use `node.start_mark.name` consistently with the success path above).","files":null}]}